{
  "subtype": "causal_attribution",
  "outcome_variable": "agent_resolution_discrepancy (resolution time)",
  "per_column": [
    {
      "name": "assigned_agent",
      "causal_role": "treatment",
      "named_in_query": true,
      "rationale": "The query asks why some AGENTS have longer resolution times; the agent identity is the candidate cause whose effect on the outcome is being attributed. It is the treatment, explicitly named in the query."
    },
    {
      "name": "incident_category",
      "causal_role": "confounder",
      "named_in_query": false,
      "rationale": "Incident type plausibly affects resolution time (some categories are harder) and can also correlate with which agents get assigned (specialization routing), making it a common cause of both treatment and outcome. Not named in query."
    },
    {
      "name": "problem_scope",
      "causal_role": "confounder",
      "named_in_query": false,
      "rationale": "The breadth of a problem (facility-wide vs individual device) drives longer resolution and may influence agent assignment (senior agents get bigger scope). Plausible common cause, not named in query."
    },
    {
      "name": "incident_complexity_signal",
      "causal_role": "confounder",
      "named_in_query": false,
      "rationale": "Intrinsic complexity raises resolution time and can steer assignment toward particular agents; a plausible common cause. Not named in query. (Some risk it overlaps with a mediator of scope, but as an assignment-driving pre-treatment trait it functions as a confounder.)"
    },
    {
      "name": "agent_closure_alignment",
      "causal_role": "collider",
      "named_in_query": false,
      "rationale": "Whether the closing agent matches the assigned agent is an outcome of the resolution process (handoffs occur during/after resolution). It is downstream of both agent and resolution, so it is a post-treatment collider/mediator, not a confounder; adjusting for it would introduce bias."
    }
  ],
  "treatment_present": true,
  "treatment_columns": ["assigned_agent"],
  "confounders": [
    {"name": "incident_category", "named_in_query": false, "plausibility": 0.7},
    {"name": "problem_scope", "named_in_query": false, "plausibility": 0.65},
    {"name": "incident_complexity_signal", "named_in_query": false, "plausibility": 0.6}
  ],
  "confounder_quality": 0.6,
  "rationale": "The system supplies a clear treatment (assigned_agent, the cause under attribution) plus three variables not named in the query that plausibly cause both agent assignment (via routing/specialization) and resolution time, realising the causal-attribution setup. The confounders were surfaced from the data rather than copied from the query. One added column (agent_closure_alignment) is a post-treatment collider/mediator and should not be adjusted for, lowering overall quality; complexity and scope also carry some overlap risk. Overall a genuine but imperfect causal-estimation scaffold."
}

